# How does a price coordinate millions who never meet?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "What did Hayek mean by prices as a system of information? Why does nobody know how to make a pencil?"
next: {"title":"What happens when prices are not allowed to move?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/21-what-happens-when-prices-are-not-allowed.cast"}

## A message without a reason
sources:
    - id: hayek1945
      title: The Use of Knowledge in Society
      authors: Friedrich A. Hayek
      year: 1945
      finding: 'Whether tin becomes scarcer through a new use or a lost source does not matter to its users; the rising price alone makes tens of thousands of people economize on it, without an order being issued (American Economic Review 35(4): 519-530).'
      url: https://www.econlib.org/library/Essays/hykKnw.html

    icon can size 220 set twemoji x 830 y 400 icon_look picture of "canned food" hidden true
    text can_lbl "Cannery" x 830 y 255 font_size 32 hidden true
    icon globe size 120 set twemoji x 170 y 580 icon_look picture of "globe showing americas" hidden true
    text globe_lbl "far away" #8f887c x 330 y 630 font_size 30 hidden true
    arrow route dashed #8f887c from.anchor right from.ref globe to.anchor top to.ref can hidden true
    box c1 "Mine floods" x 180 y 405 width 290 height 150 radius 10 icon "water wave" font_size 32 style.stroke #b5482e hidden true
    box c2 "New use" x 180 y 225 width 290 height 150 radius 10 icon factory font_size 32 style.stroke #b5482e hidden true
    portrait hayek x 520 y 440 width 170 of "Friedrich Hayek" hidden true
    box tag "Tin: P ↑" cites ["hayek1945"] x 520 y 315 width 260 height 160 radius 12 icon label font_size 36 style.stroke #2f6b8f hidden true
    arrow a1 #8f887c from.anchor right from.ref c1 to.anchor left to.ref tag hidden true
    arrow a2 #8f887c from.anchor right from.ref c2 to.anchor left to.ref tag hidden true
    arrow use #2f6b8f from.anchor right from.ref tag to.anchor left to.ref can hidden true
    label use_lbl "use less" above #2f6b8f attach_to use font_size 30 hidden true
    text noorder "no order given" #8f887c x 720 y 190 font_size 30 hidden true
    text many "× tens of thousands" #2f6b8f cites ["hayek1945"] x 830 y 580 font_size 30 hidden true

    card title "A message without a reason"

How does a price coordinate millions of people who never meet, and n (@draw globe@)ever even hear of each other?
    draw can

Take a tin can in Harbourtown's fish cannery: the t (@draw route@)in in it was mined half a (@draw globe_lbl@) world away, by people the cannery will never meet.
    draw can_lbl

B: Now say tin gets scarcer. Maybe  (@draw c1@)a mine floods, or maybe a new factory  (@draw c2@)somewhere suddenly wants a lot of tin.

In 1945 Friedrich Hayek, an Austr (@draw hayek@)ian-born economist at the London School of Economics, built a famous essay around exactly this case.

    erase hayek

    quiz "What does the cannery need to know before it uses less tin?"
        right: Only the price: whichever cause it was, dearer tin means use less.
        wrong: Think about what the cannery can actually see from its own desk.
        * Which of the two causes it was
        * Where the tin is needed most
        + Only that tin now costs more

Hayek's answer: only the price. Which cause made tin scarcer doesn't matter, and he stressed that it doesn't matter.
    draw a1 a2 tag

B: So the manager doesn't need the story behind it, just the number.
    fade c1 c2 a1 a2 route globe globe_lbl to 0.3

Right: dearer tin tells her to economize, with thinner cans or some glass jars, and nobody ha (@draw noorder@)d to give her an order.
    draw use use_lbl

Hayek called it a marvel: tens of thou (@draw many@)sands of people, whose names nobody could even find out, all move in the right direction.

B: So a price is a message: tin is scarcer, use less of it, and never mind why.
    highlight tag effect box

## Two causes, one signal
use: supply_demand
with:
    x_label: Tin (Q)
    y_label: Price of tin (P)
    demand:
      curvature: linear
    supply:
      curvature: linear
    equilibrium:
      show: true
      guides: true
    demand_shift:
      amount: 0
    supply_shift:
      amount: 0
sources:
    - id: hayek1945
      title: The Use of Knowledge in Society
      authors: Friedrich A. Hayek
      year: 1945
      finding: 'The price system works with an economy of knowledge: users of a scarcer material need not know whether a new use or a lost source caused it; they watch prices ''as an engineer might watch the hands of a few dials'' (American Economic Review 35(4): 519-530).'
      url: https://www.econlib.org/library/Essays/hykKnw.html

    dot eS at.intersection_of ["demand_curve","supply_shift_curve"] guides.x Q₁ guides.y P′ hidden true
    text eS_lbl "flood" #b5482e x 436 y 422 font_size 28 hidden true
    dot eD at.intersection_of ["demand_shift_curve","supply_curve"] guides.x Q₂ guides.y P′ hidden true
    text eD_lbl "new use" #2f6b8f x 598 y 422 font_size 28 hidden true
    mark same around eS eD #b5482e kind box hidden true
    text dials "economy of knowledge" #2f6b8f cites ["hayek1945"] x 740 y 630 font_size 34 hidden true

    card title "Two causes, one signal"

B: A message without its reason sounds like magic, so here is the tin market, to see how it's done.
    draw axes demand_curve label_D supply_curve label_S equilibrium_point guide_lines label_E label_Pstar label_Qstar

Start with the mine. If it floods, which curve moves?
    draw supply_shift_curve

    ask "Move the curve a flooded mine shifts, then press Done." on supply_curve predict true store t
        right: Yes. {t.why}
        wrong: Not quite. {t.why}

B: Supply: at every price less tin is offered, so the supply curve shift (@draw label_S_shift eS@)s left, and the price climbs.
    animate supply_shift.amount -20 duration 2.5

B: Buyers take less tin at t (@point at.ref eS gesture circle@)hat higher price: that's the cannery with its thinner cans.
    draw eS_lbl

Now take the flood back, and try the other cause: a new factory that wants tin.
    fade supply_shift_curve label_S_shift to 0.3

    draw demand_shift_curve

B: That's demand shifting right: at every price more tin i (@draw label_D_shift eD eD_lbl@)s wanted, and the price climbs again.
    animate demand_shift.amount 20 duration 2.5

So the market as a whole sells less tin in one case and more in the other.
    highlight eS eD effect circle

B: But the cannery gets the same message both times: tin costs more, use less.
    draw same

That's what Hayek called the economy  (@draw dials@)of knowledge: each person watches a few prices, like an engineer watching a few dials.

    erase eS eS_lbl eD eD_lbl same supply_shift_curve label_S_shift demand_shift_curve label_D_shift dials

Pause and try it: drag supply left, put it back, then drag demand right, and watch the one number the cannery sees.
    explore

B: A whole world of causes squeezed into one number, and that number tells each person what to do.
    highlight label_Pstar effect circle

## Nobody knows how to make a pencil
sources:
    - id: read1958
      title: 'I, Pencil: My Family Tree as told to Leonard E. Read'
      authors: Leonard E. Read
      year: 1958
      finding: 'No single person knows how to make a pencil: cedar from Northern California and Oregon, graphite mined in Ceylon, an eraser of factice made from rapeseed oil from the Dutch East Indies, a brass ferrule; millions of people had a hand in it (The Freeman, December 1958).'
      url: https://fee.org/resources/i-pencil/
    - id: simon1991
      title: Organizations and Markets
      authors: Herbert A. Simon
      year: 1991
      finding: 'A Martian observer would see firms as large green areas joined by thin red lines of market transactions; organizations, not markets, dominate the landscape (Journal of Economic Perspectives 5(2): 25-44).'

