    card title "Nobody knows how to make a pencil"

One message passed along is neat. Chain thousands of them (@draw ferrule eraser@) together, and you get something as ordinary as a pencil.
    draw body wood lead

B: In 1958 Leonard Read wrote an essay in a pencil's own voice, 'I, Pe (@draw read_src@)ncil', with a startling claim: not a single person on earth knows how to make one.

That sounds absurd. A pencil factory makes pencils.
    highlight pencil effect box

    erase read_src

B: It puts them together. Read's ced (@draw k_cedar l_cedar@)ar came from Oregon an (@draw k_lead l_lead@)d northern California, and the graphite from Sri Lanka.

The eraser came fr (@draw k_oil l_oil@)om rapeseed oil grown in Indonesia, a (@draw k_brass l_brass@)nd the little metal band is brass, made from mined copper and zinc.

B: And behind each part, more people: loggers, miners, the sailors on the ships, the people who made their saws and trucks.
    draw axe pick ship parallel false

Read put it at millions of peopl (@draw millions@)e, hardly any of whom know each other, and nobody in charge of the whole.

    erase axe pick ship millions

B: What holds it together is prices: each of them sees only what their bit sells for and what their inputs cost.
    flow l_cedar l_lead l_oil l_brass

B: But here's my doubt. Walk into the pencil  (@draw boss@)factory, and nobody there steers by prices: a ma (@draw orders@)nager tells people what to do.
    fade eraser ferrule body wood lead k_cedar k_lead k_oil k_brass l_cedar l_lead l_oil l_brass to 0.15

    hide eraser ferrule body wood lead k_cedar k_lead k_oil k_brass l_cedar l_lead l_oil l_brass boss orders

That's a fair  (@draw simon@)hit. Herbert Simon asked what a visitor  (@draw f1 t1 f2 t2 f3 t3@)from Mars would see: firms as  (@draw m12 m13 m23 t_m@)green areas, markets as thin red lines between them.

    erase simon

And to Simon's Martian, the green areas, not the red lines, were what dominated the landscape.
    highlight f1 f2 f3

B: So prices coordinate between firms, and inside them orders do; why the line falls where it does is a question of its own.
    highlight m12 m13 m23

## How far does the message reach?
feedback:
    style: dry
    perfect: Hayek would hand you a pencil.
    good: You read the dials well.
    poor: Watch what the price can and cannot see.
    none: The tin market will still be there tomorrow.
use: supply_demand
with:
    x_label: Tin (Q)
    y_label: Price of tin (P)
    demand:
      curvature: linear
    supply:
      curvature: linear
    equilibrium:
      show: true
      guides: true
      shift_label: ''
      shift_q_label: ''
      shift_p_label: P′
    supply_shift:
      amount: 0
    box:
      x: 20
      'y': 130
      w: 610
      h: 530
sources:
    - id: lange1936
      title: On the Economic Theory of Socialism
      authors: Oskar Lange
      year: 1936
      finding: A central planning board could reach equilibrium prices by trial and error, raising prices where shortages appear and lowering them where surpluses appear (Review of Economic Studies 4(1-2), 1936-37).
    - id: hayek1945
      title: The Use of Knowledge in Society
      authors: Friedrich A. Hayek
      year: 1945
      finding: 'The knowledge that matters for economic decisions is largely knowledge of the particular circumstances of time and place, dispersed among many people and never given to any single mind (American Economic Review 35(4): 519-530).'
      url: https://www.econlib.org/library/Essays/hykKnw.html

    scratch sure cites ["hayek1945","lange1936"] work ["Prices inform: consensus","Prices alone: contested"] x 820 y 450 font_size 24 hidden true
    portrait lange x 830 y 300 width 150 of "Oskar Lange" hidden true
    box dirty "River pollution: not in P" x 815 y 450 width 300 height 140 radius 10 icon factory font_size 28 style.stroke #b5482e hidden true
    scratch math work ["S falls 10% at every P",{"tex":"\\%\\Delta P \\approx 10\\% \\div (\\varepsilon_D + \\varepsilon_S)"},{"tex":"= 10\\% \\div (0.5 + 0.5) = 10\\%"}] x 820 y 450 font_size 24 hidden true

    card title "How far does the message reach?"

How sure are we? That prices carry knowledge no single person holds is (@draw sure_box sure_line_1@) about as close to consensus as economics gets.
    draw axes demand_curve label_D supply_curve label_S equilibrium_point guide_lines label_E label_Pstar label_Qstar

B: That they can do the whole job, with  (@draw sure_line_2@)no planner at all, is another matter: that's contested.

In the 1930s Oskar Lan (@draw lange@)ge argued that a planning board could find the right prices too, by trial and error, raising them wherever shortages appeared.

    erase lange

B: Hayek's reply was that what matters is knowledge of time and place, a flooded mine today, and it changes faster than any board can hear of it.
    highlight sure_line_2 effect underline

    erase sure

And a price can carry the wrong message (@draw dirty@): if a mine pollutes a river for free, the price of tin never tells anyone about the river.

    pause 0.4

    erase dirty

    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math_box"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"test"}] then test

    ask "Show the math, or skip it?" on mathq store m default "Skip it" stage own voice b

@math_box

    hide mathq

Say a flooded mine cuts the s (@animate supply_shift.amount -7 duration 2@)upply of tin by ten percent at every price.
    draw math_box math_line_1 supply_shift_curve

    draw label_S_shift shift_equilibrium_point shift_guide_lines label_P_shift

B: The price rises by roughly that shift divided by the two elasticities added together.
    draw math_line_2

With elasticities of a half each, that's ten divided by one: the price climbs about ten percent.
    draw math_line_3

    pause 0.5

    erase math supply_shift_curve label_S_shift shift_equilibrium_point shift_guide_lines label_P_shift

@test

    hide mathq

    quiz "Copper suddenly gets dearer, and an electrician switches some jobs to aluminium wire without knowing why. Which fits Hayek?"
        intro: Three new cases to finish.
        right: She is doing the right thing: dearer copper already says it is wanted more elsewhere, and why is not her problem.
        wrong: Does the right move depend on why copper got scarce?
        + She is doing the right thing without knowing the cause
        * She should wait until she knows the cause
        * Only a planner could tell her to switch

    hide axes demand_curve label_D supply_curve label_S equilibrium_point guide_lines label_E label_Pstar label_Qstar

    cards cases check each
        Prices: A mill buying cedar logs
        Orders: A foreman assigning shifts
        Prices: Graphite sold to the top bidder
        Orders: An army moving its supplies

    ask "Coordinated by prices or by orders? Sort each case." on cases store f stage own voice b
        right: All four. Between firms, prices steer; inside a firm or an army, someone gives orders.
        wrong: {f} of {f.total} on the first try. Ask: does anyone inside give the order?

    erase cases

    show axes demand_curve label_D supply_curve label_S equilibrium_point guide_lines label_E label_Pstar label_Qstar

    quiz "A factory dumps its waste into a river for free. What is wrong with the price of what it makes?" voice b
        right: It says the product is cheaper to make than it really is: the river's cost never enters the price.
        wrong: Who pays for the river, and does the price know?
        + It says the product is cheaper to make than it really is
        * Nothing: a price always carries the full cost
        * It is too high

So millions coordinate without meeting because each listens to a few prices, an (@highlight equilibrium_point label_Pstar effect pulse@)d every price is a message written by everyone else.
    animate box full duration 2.5

